# Merge behaviour for the governed documents.
#
# Twelve open PRs on this repo were all CONFLICTING at once, and not one of them
# conflicted on code. Every collision was in the narrative files: two branches
# each added an entry at the top of CHANGELOG.md, or a row to the findings
# INDEX, and git correctly reported that it could not know which came first.
#
# That is not a merge problem, it is a missing merge strategy. `union` keeps
# BOTH sides of an overlapping hunk instead of raising a conflict, which is
# exactly right for a file whose only edit is "add my entry".
#
# The rule for putting a file on this list: it must be APPEND-ONLY in practice.
# Union never deletes, so a file that legitimately has content removed must NOT
# be here - union would silently resurrect whatever the other branch deleted.
# That is not hypothetical: a stale branch in this repo deletes the P-06 compute
# box from PARKING_LOT.md, and union-merging that file would quietly bring it
# back on one side while another branch was retiring it.
#
#   CHANGELOG.md   append-only by law, newest first        -> union
#   INDEX.md       findings table, rows are only added     -> union
#
# Deliberately NOT union, and why:
#
#   STATUS.md      state, not history. Capped at 60 lines, and items are
#                  REPLACED as they stop being true. Union would keep both
#                  versions of a superseded line and blow the cap.
#   PARKING_LOT.md entries are REMOVED when their unlock condition is met.
#   docs/ACTIVE.md a map of what is where; stale entries get deleted.
#
# Those three still conflict, on purpose: resolving them is a judgement about
# what is currently true, and a machine guessing at that is how a roadmap ends
# up asserting two contradictory things at once.

CHANGELOG.md                      merge=union
docs/subagents_findings/INDEX.md  merge=union

# Keep the repo's line endings stable across the Windows checkouts this estate
# runs on. Every `git add` here currently warns "LF will be replaced by CRLF",
# which means the index and the working tree disagree about every text file -
# and that disagreement is why `git apply --cached` refuses patches that are
# otherwise clean.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.woff2 binary
*.png   binary
